+# Reviewed inherited methods
+
+The `Override <method> (<declaring class>)` code actions propose one inherited method from a named class declaration, member, body or comment. The editor must review the insertion before applying it. The action kind is `refactor.rewrite.overrideMethod`; the existing `Implement ...` quick fix retains its title and kind and proposes all required methods together.
+
+Concrete inherited methods delegate to `parent::method(...)`. Parameter references, variadic forwarding, static and reference-returning methods retain the actual declaration syntax. Void methods do not return the call result. Never-returning methods delegate and throw if the parent unexpectedly returns. Abstract, interface and direct-trait methods use an explicit `LogicException` placeholder rather than pretending that an implementation exists.
+
+Generation uses the shared inherited-member collection, class lookup, subtype rules and signature formatters. Syntax from the actual declaring method supplies native types, references and defaults. Lexical `self` and `parent` types and default class names keep their declaring context across namespaces; imports in the editing file are used only when they resolve to the same class. Existing PHPDoc types do not become new native restrictions. Unknown generic type arguments and complete documentation inheritance are not synthesized by this action.
+
+The target class must be closed and parse without errors. The caret may be in a member, method body or comment within that class; the edit always goes immediately before its validated closing brace. HTML and anonymous-class contexts are unavailable, and the smallest containing class is selected so a nested anonymous class cannot accidentally generate into its outer class. Existing methods, private and final inherited methods, magic methods and synthesized members are excluded from overrides. The implementation quick fix can still supply required magic methods. Layout follows the target indentation and line endings. Multiline string defaults are encoded from their parsed bytes so editor line-ending normalization cannot change their value.
+
+Generated signatures must satisfy the known native contracts throughout the parent, interface and trait hierarchy, including parameter variance, references, required/variadic counts, static/visibility and return types. Conflicting parameter names are refused because silently picking one would change named-argument behavior. The action refuses rather than synthesizes a merged signature for incompatible inherited requirements. PHP target checks cover the native syntax being emitted; an override attribute is generated only from PHP 8.3 and only for an actual parent/interface override.
+
+Some source forms cannot yet be transported safely. Trait precedence/alias adaptations are not rewritten into an explicit override signature. Magic-constant and document-string defaults, inaccessible private default constants/constructors, unavailable source syntax and unresolved constant defaults are refused. Class/interface conflict synthesis, generalized trait adaptation, magic-method override generation, full style/version/corpus acceptance and complete dependency epochs remain open. This is bounded method generation, not complete code-generation acceptance.
+
+Code-action requests prepare pending open PHP source revisions before resolving inherited signatures. An unsaved edit or undo in a parent file is therefore available to the first request in an unchanged child, without waiting for background diagnostics. This uses the existing semantic preparation boundary; changes arriving during a request still invalidate its result.
